Introduction
The D&E Adhesives Competence Group develops knowledge on adhesives including adhesive properties, processing, modeling, way of working with adhesives, and much more. This knowledge results in a robust, reliable and predictable adhesive bond connection, aimed to ensure specifications and regulations on all levels.
Your Assignment
Last December, the EU announced a ban of Bisphenol-A (BPA) products for food contact application due to their harmful impact on human health system. This ban might be extended in the future to other classes of BPA-containing materials, including structural epoxy. If that would be the case, some of the adhesives employed in ASML could be discontinued. Given that at ASML safety, health, and wellness of people are taken seriously and compliance with European regulations is required, ASML is eager to start looking into possible alternatives to this class of compounds. Therefore, the main tasks of this internship include:
Scouting of possible commercial alternatives to BPA-Containing adhesives, like MMA Adhesives;
Selection of BPA-free adhesives, taking into account the health aspect as well as mechanical and chemical properties;
Testing the selected adhesives for cleanliness;
Testing the selected adhesives for mechanical properties in our cleanroom laboratory (lap shear and tensile strength, dynamic mechanical properties, fatigue and creep behavior);
Comparison of the results with the BPA-based adhesives, and highlight the advantages and disadvantages of using one type or the other.
This is a Bachelor or Master apprenticeship or graduation (thesis) internship with a desired duration of 6 months or more, working 5 days per week, 3 days onsite. The start date is around September - October 2025.
Your profile
To be a fit for this assignment, you:
Are busy with your bachelor's or master's in Chemical or Mechanical Engineering, or Materials Science;
Have a solid knowledge of polymer, coating chemistry and physical properties and concepts;
Have some experience with mechanical testing tools (DMA, Dynamometer, etc.) would be a plus;
Have a hands-on mentality and willingness to perform practical work in the lab;
Have a good communication skills in English (verbal and written).
